\begin{question}
\textbf{Simulating from a data-generating process}

The monthly electricity costs $Y_i$ (in EUR) and the living space $X_i$ (in m$^2$) of households are generated by the DGP
$$
Y_i = \beta_1 + \beta_2 X_i + \varepsilon_i,\qquad \varepsilon_i\overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2),
$$
with $\beta_1=30$, $\beta_2=0.8$, $\sigma=15$, and $X_i\sim\mathcal{N}(80, 20^2)$ independent of $\varepsilon_i$.

\begin{parts}
\item Simulate $n=200$ households from this DGP.
\item Draw a scatter plot and add the true regression line $\beta_1+\beta_2x$ with \texttt{abline()}.
\item Split the simulated households into three groups: $X_i\leq 60$, $60<X_i\leq 100$ and $X_i>100$. Compute the mean of $X_i$ and the mean of $Y_i$ within each group and add these three points to your plot. What do you observe?
\item Compute the sample correlation between $X_i$ and $Y_i$. Then repeat the simulation with $\sigma=40$. How do the scatter plot and the correlation change?
\end{parts}
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
\begin{parts}
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
beta1 <- 30; beta2 <- 0.8; sigma <- 15
n   <- 200
X   <- rnorm(n, mean = 80, sd = 20)
eps <- rnorm(n, mean = 0, sd = sigma)
Y   <- beta1 + beta2 * X + eps
@
\item The plot below also contains the group means from (c).
\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.6\textwidth}
<<dgpplot, echo=TRUE, fig=TRUE, height=4, width=6, eps=FALSE>>=
plot(X, Y, pch = 19, col = "gray",
     xlab = "Living space (m^2)", ylab = "Electricity costs (EUR)")
abline(a = beta1, b = beta2, lwd = 2, lty = 2)

## group means (alternatively: mean(Y[X <= 60]), etc.)
group  <- cut(X, breaks = c(-Inf, 60, 100, Inf))
mean_X <- tapply(X, group, mean)
mean_Y <- tapply(Y, group, mean)
points(mean_X, mean_Y, pch = 19, col = "red", cex = 1.8)
@
\item The group means are computed in the code of (b):
<<echo=TRUE>>=
cbind(mean_X, mean_Y)
@
The three group means (red points) lie close to the true regression line: the group means of $Y_i$ estimate the conditional means $E(Y_i\mid X_i=x)$ at the group means $x$ of $X_i$.
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
cor(X, Y)
Y_noisy <- beta1 + beta2 * X + rnorm(n, mean = 0, sd = 40)
cor(X, Y_noisy)
@
With $\sigma=40$, the points scatter much more around the (same) regression line, and the correlation is clearly smaller. The regression function $E(Y_i\mid X_i=x)$ is unchanged; only the noise is larger.
\end{parts}
\end{solution}

